                     "topFinding":  "The v0.7.2/v0.7.3 releases closed real ground since the last review: both HIGH dependency CVEs are patched, the three unbatched multi-statement D1 writes that drove reliability to 44 are fixed with db.batch() and migration 0012\u0027s UNIQUE(shift_id,user_id) constraint, and two chronic doc-drift findings are resolved. But the same bug class recurred one level down: the time-off-approval day_unavailability cascade is still an unbatched per-day loop, so an interruption mid-approval can leave a request marked approved with only part of its date range blocked from scheduling -- this alone keeps the verdict at needs-attention. The strategic-kill-this findings are now chronic at 8 runs: the drive-to-first-real-user-vs-payment-integration decision has sat unmade since 2026-06-28 while four releases of adjacent quality work shipped instead. Two skill-suite findings surfaced as HIGH and are portfolio-level rather than Scheduler-specific, but they block clean automated review going forward.",
